# THE BUILTIN TRANSLITERATION MAP (V26), in the same grammar a user writes
# and compiled in with `include_str!` (`src/charset:V22`).
#
# Both sides are written `U+XXXX` wherever the character is not ASCII, so
# this file is pure ASCII and needs no grant to pass the check it feeds.
# An ASCII replacement is written as itself where that is legible (`--`)
# and as a code point where it is not (a space, a quote).
#
# WHAT THIS IS FOR, measured rather than assumed: R4 found that the
# typography map ALONE settles 45.1% of the fleet's non-ASCII files. These
# are characters a writer did not choose -- an editor or a word processor
# substituted them -- so rewriting them takes nothing away.
#
# WHAT IT IS NOT: a default grant. `typography` is a SET a file may ask
# for (`src/charset`), and a file that asks keeps its real typography
# because `fix` never touches an allowed character (V6). The map decides
# what happens to a character that is NOT allowed, which is the only
# question it is ever asked.

# EM DASH and EN DASH. The em dash doubles because a single hyphen where a
# sentence breaks reads as a word join.
U+2014 --
U+2013 -

# MINUS SIGN, which is a dash a keyboard cannot type and arithmetic does
# not need: the ASCII hyphen is what every compiler and calculator reads.
U+2212 -

# The curly quotes, all four, to their straight forms. LEFT and RIGHT
# SINGLE QUOTATION MARK to APOSTROPHE; LEFT and RIGHT DOUBLE to QUOTATION
# MARK.
U+2018 U+0027
U+2019 U+0027
U+201C U+0022
U+201D U+0022

# The LOW-9 and REVERSED-9 quotes, which open a quotation in Polish,
# German, Czech and their neighbours and are closed by the curly ones
# above (B73): SINGLE LOW-9 and SINGLE HIGH-REVERSED-9 QUOTATION MARK to
# APOSTROPHE; DOUBLE LOW-9 and DOUBLE HIGH-REVERSED-9 to QUOTATION MARK.
# Without them `fix` straightened only the closing quote and left a
# half-converted quotation that `check` still refused.
U+201A U+0027
U+201B U+0027
U+201E U+0022
U+201F U+0022

# The angle quotes used as quotation marks in several languages, to the
# straight double. A locale that wants its own quotation marks grants
# them; per-locale punctuation is `typography-<code>`, deferred until T32
# vendors CLDR.
U+00AB U+0022
U+00BB U+0022

# HORIZONTAL ELLIPSIS to three full stops, which is what it abbreviates.
U+2026 ...

# NO-BREAK SPACE to an ordinary space. The replacement is `U+0020` and not
# a trailing blank: the line is split on whitespace, so a space nobody can
# see would be a poor way to declare one.
U+00A0 U+0020

# DELETED, which is a replacement written as nothing at all -- the one way
# this crate ever removes a character (V4), and explicit for that reason.
#
# ZERO WIDTH SPACE and BYTE ORDER MARK carry no glyph, so there is nothing
# to transliterate them INTO: any visible replacement would put a
# character on the page that the author never typed. Both are also
# hazards (`src/lint/hazard:V34`) when they turn up where nobody expects them,
# which is the other half of why they leave rather than change.
U+200B
U+FEFF

# EMOJI MODIFIERS, deleted (`src/fix/emoji:V60`). The `emoji` preset grants
# single code points only (`src/fix/emoji:V31`), so a thumbs-up with a skin
# tone, or a check mark with the emoji presentation selector, always carries one
# code point the preset withholds. Deleting the modifier leaves the base emoji,
# which the preset grants: thumbs-up medium skin tone becomes thumbs-up. This is
# the cheap half of `src/fix/emoji:V31` and needs no sequence scanning; ZWJ
# sequences, keycaps and flags still wait for `src/fix/emoji:T33`.
#
# VARIATION SELECTOR-15 and -16 (text and emoji presentation), then the
# five EMOJI MODIFIER FITZPATRICK skin tones.
U+FE0E
U+FE0F
U+1F3FB
U+1F3FC
U+1F3FD
U+1F3FE
U+1F3FF
